    "available_at": "2026-10-15T21:01:46.827215+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nTomplan Does Toy Hauler Campers Differently – Composite Midi Cargo XL Weighs As Little as 772 Lbs\n15 photos\nPhoto: Micro Camper\nHeading out into virgin ground and not knowing what you’ll find while exploring the wilderness is quite attractive, with the downside being that you can only cover so much ground in the couple days you’re out there. So, the industry spat out the toy hauler, and today, we explore a rather special and unique option.\nIt’s called the Midi Cargo XL, and while it may look like an AT-ST (Chicken Walker) out of Star Wars and even stores little humans inside, it serves a completely opposite purpose other than intergalactic warfare; this one’s all about the leisure side of life and designed to carry some of our toys to unexplored corners of our planet. But there are some catches to the story, and that’s what we’re here to find out.\nPhoto: Micro Camper First and foremost, we need to consider what sort of trailer we’re working with here, and using the word toy hauler, even though that’s what it is, may have you thinking about tossing an ATV inside, and while there is room for one in terms of cargo-carrying capacity, the lack of large rear door and ramp limits what sort of cargo we can stuff inside.\nThis leaves us with things like kayaks, e-bikes, fishing and climbing gear, and outdoor tents and dining sets. There are 400 kg (882 lbs) of cargo wiggle room for your gear and standard, or in its Basic layout, the Cargo XL weighs around 350 kg (772 lbs) and has a GVWR of 750 kg (1,653 lbs).\nWhile it may not sound like much, there is a reason why Tomplan Trailers, the hearts and hands behind the design limit the GVWR : in Europe, it allows vehicle owners to tow without any special driver’s license classes, as long as the vehicle and trailer together roll in under 3,500 kg.\nPhoto: Tomplan More on the storage side of things, there is an outdoor hatch that allows us to stuff gear under the bed, and more under-bed storage is granted by a drawer, accessible from inside. Do take note that the basic Cargo XL is a completely empty shell, and includes no bed, drawers, creature comforts, heater, or any other camping features. The Full model is the one with goodies inside.\nWith all the camper features inside, or the Full model, the Cargo XL weighs around an extra 100 kg (220 lbs), leaving us with 300 kg (662 lbs) to work with, plenty for a couple of e-bikes, and other essentials.\nHow is such a low weight possible? By how the Cargo XL is built. At its core, Tomplan used an all- composite makeup with a foam core for its shell, providing thermal insulation and rigidity while keeping things light. The Full does include an electric heater inside to keep things comfortable outside of summer temperatures.\nPhoto: Tomplan Other features we find inside which make up the Full version include a fridge, the furniture we see lining the upper level of the camper, and 230 V and 12 V sockets and electrical, and the sink with faucet. Only a portable toilet can be brought along on our outings, so we’ll need to grab a portable shower or find campgrounds with amenities.\nNow, if we take all this and place ourselves in the middle, we have can tap into an experience that may not be ready for prolonged off-grid use, but the few days we can spend out there will still be filled with mornings where the only sounds you hear are the leaves rustling in the trees or a brook babbling about nearby.\nFrom here, you lean over to your significant other, give them a kiss on the cheek, and then climb out of bed, open the rear door, step out into the toilet annex you added outside, and do your bidding. After, wash those hands and put a pot of coffee on the gas top you brought along.\nPhoto: Micro Camper Since the smell of roasted coffee has also lifted your significant other from his or her slumber, something your magical kiss couldn’t do, you both head back outside where you plop down on a couple of chairs to take in the sights and sounds of today’s morning. Stay there for a bit, but don’t forget about breakfast; you have some bike riding, kayaking, or fishing to do today.\nThe rest is up to your imagination, but there is a bit of room for improvement, if you ask me. What I can see lacking here is a more equipped electrical system, but this can easily be resolved with a portable power bank and some solar panels, and these days, there are plenty to choose from. EcoFlow and Jackery are some very often-called-upon brands with a great reputation.\nConsidering that you will have plenty pf cash left over after your Midi Cargo XL acquisition, you should be fine. I mean with some dealers selling a Full version for as low as €8,500 (without local taxes) is quite the deal. That’s the equivalent to $9,500, marking it as one of the most inexpensive toy hauler campers I've covered this year.\nThe downside to Tomplan goodies, at least, pertaining to North Americans, is that this brand doesn’t sell in the US. If you do want to get your hands on one, you’ll need to cover shipping and import fees, not to mention modifications to make it road legal in the US, if it’s not.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntravel trailer camper trailer Toy Hauler cargo trailer lightweight affordable composite\nAbout the author: Cristian Curmei\nA bit of a nomad at heart (being born in Europe and raised in several places in the USA), Cristian is enamored with travel trailers, campers and bikes. He also tests and writes about urban means of transportation like scooters, mopeds and e-bikes (when he's not busy hosting our video stories and guides). Full profile\nUP NEXT I Drove All of the Six New Cars in Gran Turismo 7 Around the Latest Circuit in the Game",
